  "id": "COMPOUND:537 - PROTEIN:1386",
  "verbalization": "Acetohydroxamic acid is implicated in the relationship with Neutrophil collagenase through a multi-step pathway involving G-protein coupled estrogen receptor 1. The drug acts as a target for multiple compounds, including (2S)-4-Hydroxy-2-(2-Hydroxypropan-2-Yl)-5-(3-Methylbutanoyl)-9-Phenyl-2,3-Dihydrofuro[2,3-F]Chromen-7-One and S-Methyl-N-{4-[(Alpha-L-Rhamnosyloxy)Benzyl]}Thiocarbamate, both of which ultimately target Neutrophil collagenase. This suggests that acetohydroxamic acid may influence Neutrophil collagenase through indirect pathways, possibly by modulating the activity of these receptor-targeted compounds. The evidence highlights a potential mechanism where acetohydroxamic acid could act as a bridge, facilitating interactions with other molecules that in turn affect Neutrophil collagenase. The integration of these pathways underscores the complexity of the relationship, emphasizing the role of receptor-targeted compounds in mediating the drug's effects on Neutrophil collagenase.",
